  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe 07Z water vapor imagery showed the center of the upper ridge \nover the central Rockies with the mean westerlies across the northern\nplains and into the Great Lakes. Surface obs showed a weak surface \npattern remained over the central plains favoring light southerly \nflow. \n\nFor today models show the upper ridge expanding east with height \nrises across the state. This is expected to help mid level temps \nwarm with 700MB readings of 13C or better progged to spread over the \nforecast area this afternoon. This should increase inhibition for \nsurface parcels marginally while the upper ridge favors general \nsubsidence. So think we should avoid the afternoon pop up showers \ntoday. If a shower were to form, it most likely would be across east \ncentral KS where the influence of the upper ridging and inhibition \nshould be weaker. Operational solutions continue to support highs in \nthe upper 90s and around 100 degrees. Still the main question for the\nheat advisory is how much dewpoints will mix out this afternoon. And\nmodels don't really show much of a different story from the past \ncouple days. Dewpoints across north central KS have held in the upper\n60s the last couple afternoons. With a light southwest wind possibly\nhelping a little deeper mixing, have dewpoints falling into the \nmiddle 60s by the late afternoon. Still heat indices should be near \n105. And for the eastern counties where dewpoints should remain in \nthe lower 70s, there is reasonable confidence for heat indices to be \naround 105 degrees. So haven't made any chances to the advisory for \ntoday. \n\nTuesday should see a frontal boundary push south through the day \nwith winds becoming north and northeast. However there isn't a lot \nof cold air with this boundary and models want to pool some moisture \nbehind the front. Because of this highs should still make the mid \nand upper 90s along and south of the KS river. With the moisture \nalong the boundary, heat indices could be near 105 through the mid-\nafternoon for Anderson and Coffey counties. So went ahead with an \nadvisory for those two counties Tuesday. Models still show some warm \nair at 700MB with no real wave to weaken the capping inversion. So \nthe boundary is forecast to move through the area dry. \n\nWednesday through Friday should see the upper ridge shift over the \nsouthern plains with shortwave energy influencing the forecast area. \nModels continue to point towards a better defined vort max rounding \nthe upper ridge for Wednesday night and Thursday with a second wave \nwithin the northwest flow for Friday. The NBM has come in with \nhigher POPs for these times and don't see a reason to change them. \nThe ensembles tend to have the greatest spread in temps for Friday \nand into the weekend as they struggle with how quickly to weaken the\nsurface ridge and bring return flow back into the central plains. \nWith a reasonable chance for precip through Friday, think the NBM is \non track with keeping highs in the 80s. \n\nFor the weekend, the upper ridge is progged to re-amplify over the \ncentral Rockies pushing the westerlies back north of the forecast \narea. This should enable a dry and hot weather pattern to redevelop.",
